    32	It proves return value `ref(2)`, final `heapLoc` 3, and these three allocations:
    33	
    34	```text
    35	0 |-> list(rowsAppend(.ValSeq, RS, X, 0))
    36	1 |-> list(sortKeyVS(rowsAppend(.ValSeq, RS, X, 0), COLUMNCLOSURE))
    37	2 |-> list(sortKeyVS(
    38	              sortKeyVS(rowsAppend(.ValSeq, RS, X, 0), COLUMNCLOSURE),
    39	              ROWCLOSURE))
    40	```
    41	
    42	The supporting claims are `SPEC.inner-loop`, `SPEC.outer-loop`,
    43	`SPEC.column-key`, and `SPEC.row-key`.
    44	
    45	## Proof-extension inventory
    46	
    47	### Definitional summaries
    48	
    49	| Extension | Domain and equations | Role, context, state, and justification | Dependents and validation |
    50	|---|---|---|---|
    51	| `rowContents(Val)` | Total: `list(VS) => VS`; every non-list value gives `.ValSeq` by the disjoint `owise` equation. | Pure value projection. It does not rewrite program execution or access cells. | `listRows`, the `For` bridge, `rowsAppend`; equation coverage and overlap audited. |
    52	| `listRows(ValSeq)` | Total structural recursion: empty is true; a head `V` is accepted exactly when `V ==K list(rowContents(V))`, followed by the recursive tail check. | Formal input-shape predicate. It reads no operational state and affects only theorem applicability. | `SPEC.outer-loop`, `SPEC.get-row`; witnesses `.ValSeq` and `vCons(list(vCons(5,.ValSeq)),.ValSeq)` satisfy it. |
    53	| `advanceIndex(Int, ValSeq)` | Total: empty returns the accumulator; a cons increments once and recurses on the tail. | Solver-friendly exact count of loop iterations. No execution is replaced. | Both loop claims; structurally descending and exhaustive. |
    54	| `scanAppend(ValSeq, ValSeq, Int, Int, Int)` | Total: empty returns the accumulator. A cons either appends `(row,column)` or does not, under the disjoint guards `V ==K X` and `notBool (V ==K X)`, then recurses on the tail. | Exact mathematical name for the inner loop's heap value. The two recursive equations are also marked `[simplification]`; the attribute adds no equation beyond the guarded definitions. | Inner/outer/entry claims. Coverage is base plus cons, guards are complements, recursion descends, and the body mutation is rejected. |
    55	| `rowsAppend(ValSeq, ValSeq, Int, Int)` | Empty returns the accumulator. A cons equation applies under `V ==K list(rowContents(V))`, scans that row, and recurses on the tail. It is intentionally partial outside `listRows`. | Exact mathematical name for the nested scan. Its `[simplification]` attribute repeats the same truthful guarded equation. No execution is replaced. | Outer/entry claims; the formal preconditions cover every use. |
    56	
    57	`INNERBODY`, `OUTERBODY`, `GETROWBODY`, `COLUMNCLOSURE`, and `ROWCLOSURE`
    58	are compile-time macros. They expand to source constructors and do not remain
    59	as runtime rewrites.
    60	
    61	### Operational bridge
    62	
    63	The only proof-local execution rewrite is:
    64	
    65	```k
    66	rule <k> For(T:Expr, V:Val, B:Stmts)
    67	      => #loop(list(rowContents(V)), T, B) ... </k>
    68	     requires V ==K list(rowContents(V))
    69	     [priority(40)]
    70	```
    71	
    72	- Class: operational bridge (one composed `For` setup step).
    73	- Complete matched context: `For(T,V,B)` at the head of `<k>`, arbitrary
    74	  continuation admitted by `...`, and every other configuration cell framed.
    75	- Justification scope: `SHAPE-CONNECTION-SPEC.for-list-shape` has the same
    76	  arbitrary target, body, continuation, and framed cells, with the same guard.
    77	  Its definition imports `ROW-MODEL` and fixed `MPY`, not `VERIFICATION`, so it
