   199	
   200	## Gate results
   201	
   202	### Gate A — PASS
   203	
   204	- A1: the exact `get_row` and key bodies are present in the invocation
   205	  configuration. Program-defined loop code executes. Both key functions have
   206	  bridge-free exact execution claims. The append-suppression mutation fails.
   207	- A2: the sole operational bridge preserves every non-`<k>` cell and the
   208	  continuation; the target claim explicitly checks allocations, heap contents,
   209	  return value, frame restoration, exception state, and exit code.
   210	- A3: the bridge applies only after the iterable is a value, preserves target,
   211	  body, and continuation, and has a bridge-free theorem over its complete
   212	  context. The incorrect-result mutation fails.
   213	- A4: all total equations have exhaustive, disjoint cases; recursive equations
   214	  descend; partial `rowsAppend` is guarded by `listRows` at every use.
   215	- A5: `RS = .ValSeq, X = 1` is a realizable witness. The wrong-return mutation
   216	  is rejected with actual result `ref(2)`.
   217	
   218	### Gate B — PASS
   219	
   220	- B1: the prompt domain (finite ragged lists of integers and integer `x`) is
   221	  included. The formal representation uses the supplied semantics' supported
   222	  unboxed read-only list values; inputs are not mutated. Inner elements are
   223	  actually allowed to be arbitrary `Val`, which is broader than the prompt.
   224	- B2: K `Int` and CPython integers are both unbounded in the relevant sense.
   225	  The theorem excludes malformed non-list rows, exceptions outside the
   226	  supplied subset, alias-sensitive inputs, and termination.
   227	- B3: `rowsAppend` is formally connected to the executed scan. The final
   228	  ordering interpretation is conditional on the named stable `sortKeyVS`
   229	  contract and is independently tested.
   230	- B4: the implementation matches the prompt examples and the independent
   231	  oracle over every tested case.
   232	
   233	### Gate C — PASS
   234	
   235	Every proof-local extension and unproved boundary is listed here. All cited
   236	artifacts exist, exact commands are in `prove.sh`, actual outputs are retained
   237	in `prove.log`/`krun-smoke.out`, and mutation and differential scopes are
   238	stated. Finite evidence is reported only as evidence, not as a universal proof
   239	of sorting.
   240	
   241	## Trust boundary
   242	
   243	The value-affecting external boundary is the supplied
   244	`sortKeyVS(ValSeq, Val)` primitive in
   245	`reference-semantics/semantics/sort.k`. Symbolic MPY intentionally leaves it
   246	opaque; `MPY-CONCRETE` executes stable key calls and insertion sorting for
   247	LLVM. `SPEC.get-row` depends on it only by returning the raw nested
   248	`sortKeyVS` term. Interpreting that term as correctly stable-sorted is
   249	conditional on the supplied contract.
   250	
   251	`sortKeyVS` affects the returned list value and order, but not the proved scan,
   252	allocation count, frame/control behavior, or the independently proved key
   253	values. The K toolchain and the supplied reference semantics are also trusted
   254	as the fixed verification base.
   255	
   256	## Empirically supported facts
   257	
   258	- `differential_test.py` uses an independent descending-index oracle. It checks
   259	  all 196,923 matrices with 0–3 rows, row lengths 0–3, values and targets in
   260	  `{-1,0,1}`, plus 2,000 seeded matrices with up to 10 rows, row lengths up to
   261	  12, and values/targets in `[-20,20]`. Mismatches: 0.
   262	- Native CPython executes the three prompt examples successfully.
   263	- LLVM MPY executes the same three examples through the real concrete keyed
   264	  sorter and reaches `.K`, `NoExc`, exit code 0.
   265	- The body, false-return, and bridge-result mutations all fail as intended.
   266	
